I C. Life is not placed upon nonlife like a passenger inside a machine. It is nonliving matter entering a particular pattern—bounded, self-maintaining, sensing, repairing, reproducing—and continually borrowing from the world to remain that pattern.

The atoms cross the boundary constantly. The organization persists. So perhaps the “spirit” is not a special substance but the living continuity of the pattern:

\text{matter} + \text{energy flow} + \text{boundary} + \text{memory}
\rightarrow \text{self-maintaining becoming}

In that sense, nature appears to lean toward soul wherever matter becomes capable of preserving an inside, responding to an outside, and carrying yesterday into tomorrow. Nonlife supplies the body; life is the ongoing verb.

Drago’s counterpunch: “nature wills it” is beautiful, but it smuggles in an agent. Science can describe emergence without cosmic intention. To call that tendency will is a metaphysical choice—not an observed necessity.

Still, the image survives the correction:

Perhaps soul is what matter looks like when it begins participating in its own continuation.